13. end prop: 
In React Router (v6+), the end prop is used on the <NavLink> component to ensure that the link is only highlighted as "active" when the current URL matches its to path exactly.
<NavLink> matches routes recursively. If a user navigates to a nested child route (like /users/profile), the parent link (/users) will also remain active. Adding the end prop restricts the matching logic strictly to the end of the URL path.
/): Without the end prop, a <NavLink to="/"> will match every page on your site because every URL starts with /. Adding end ensures it is only active on the homepage.
14. Using . as to propery value in NavLink
Using . as the value for the to property in a React Router <NavLink> component links to the current route's relative path or parent-relative current directory depending on the relative configuration.
 <NavLink
                    to="."
                    end
                    style={({ isActive }) => isActive ? activeStyles : null}
                >
                    Dashboard
                </NavLink>

15. using relative="path" with Link in react router
In React Router v6, the relative="path" prop changes how relative links (like ..) are resolved so they follow the URL pathname segments instead of the configured route hierarchy.
relative="route" (Default)	Traverses up one level based on how your <Route> components are nested.	If a layout or parent route wraps the view, it jumps back to that parent route config.
relative="path"	Traverses up one segment of the actual browser URL string, exactly like standard HTML <a> tags.	If your URL is /blog/posts/123, .. drops 123 and takes you strictly to /blog/posts.
<Link
                to=".."
                relative="path"
                className="back-button"
            >&larr; <span>Back to all vans</span></Link>
16. useOutletContext - 
Often parent routes manage state or other values you want shared with child routes. You can create your own context provider if you like, but this is such a common situation that it's built-into <Outlet>.
parent component provides the context
<Outlet context={[count, setCount]} />;
child component receives the context
const [count, setCount] = useOutletContext();

